<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austro-Turkish War (1716–1718)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1716">1716</a>–<a href="/years/1718">1718</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Austro-Turkish War of 1716 to 1718 was fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> entered in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, then at war with the Ottomans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> won a decisive victory at Petrovaradin in 1716, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeswar</a> the same year and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1717. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> in 1718 gave Austria the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> with Belgrade, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austro-turkish-war-1716-1718.jpg" alt="Austro-Turkish War (1716–1718)" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg Philipp Rugendas the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georg-philipp-rugendas-the-elder-1716-battle-of-petrovaradin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of 1699 was not an acceptable permanent settlement for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which looked for revenge for its defeat at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683. Its army under Grand Vizier Baltacı Mehmet defeated the Russian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> in the Russo-Turkish War of 1710 to 1713. During the Ottoman–Venetian War of 1714 to 1718 Grand Vizier Damat Ali reconquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> from the Venetians. The Austrians, as guarantor of the Treaty of Karlowitz, threatened the Ottoman Empire, which declared war in April 1716.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first engagement, the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Karlowitz</a> on 2 August 1716, was an Ottoman victory. Three days later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> defeated the Turks at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-petrovaradin">Battle of Petrovaradin</a>, and in October he conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> and its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temesvár</a>. In 1717 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to approach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> from the east and rear, and the siege began on 18 June. The Ottoman garrison of 30,000 under Serasker Mustafa Pasha held the city, while a relief army of about 140,000 under Grand Vizier Halil Pasha arrived on 28 July and dug trenches instead of attacking.</p><p>Eugene's army, caught between the fortress and the relief army, was worn down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire and malaria. On 14 August a mortar shell fired from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemun">Zemun</a> struck the ammunition store inside the fortress and killed 3,000 defenders. Eugene ordered a surprise attack for the night of 15 to 16 August, and in heavy fog his troops drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> from their camp after about ten hours. Ottoman losses were put at 15,000 to 20,000 men and all 166 of their guns, against fewer than 6,000 Austrian losses. The garrison surrendered on 21 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> was signed on 21 July 1718. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> gained Lower Syrmia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temesvár</a>, the last Ottoman fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and its region, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of Temeswar, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, parts of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and the Bosnian sections of Posavina. Wallachia ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>, which became the Banat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a>. Venice was to surrender the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> but kept the Ionian Islands. Belgrade passed to the Habsburgs after 196 years of Ottoman rule; the Ottomans reconquered it in 1739.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Austro-Turkish War (1716–1718)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Austro-Turkish war of 1716 to 1718</b> Habsburg forces occupied Oltenia, the part of Wallachia west of the Olt river.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-craiova">Banat of Craiova</a></li></ul></section>
